{%- comment -%}
  sections/main-collection-lada.liquid
  Rendered by templates/collection.lada.json, which is what the Alamgeer
  and Ready to Wear collections are put on. Every other collection on the
  store keeps sections/main-collection.liquid and is untouched by this file.

  WHY THIS EXISTS AS A SEPARATE SECTION RATHER THAN A FLAG ON THE SHARED ONE.
  The shared listing prints a breadcrumb, a title, the collection
  description, a result count and a filter button. Lada prints the title.
  Wiring "hide the breadcrumb, hide the description, hide the count" into
  main-collection.liquid as five settings leaves that file owned by two
  registers and safe to edit for neither, and the first person to add a
  sixth thing to it puts it back on this page by accident.

  WHAT IS THE SAME AS main-collection.liquid, ON THE CLIENT'S EXPLICIT
  INSTRUCTION, AND MUST STAY THE SAME:
    - the <aside class="filters"> left rail, driven by collection.filters,
      same markup, same .chip, same price range, same data-filters /
      data-filter-toggle / data-filter-close / data-scrim hooks that
      assets/site.js already binds. No new JS, none needed.
    - paginate by 24
    - the product grid is .pgrid, so a Lada card sits on exactly the same
      column count and the same 4:5 plate as every other listing
    - pagination through {{ paginate | default_pagination }}
  Only the look changes: dark ground, champagne hairlines, Lada card.

  THE RAIL WAS AHEAD OF THE SHARED ONE ON THREE POINTS AND IS NOW LEVEL
  WITH IT ON ALL THREE. Worth reading before touching any of them:
    - the aside carries a real id (LadaFilters) for the phone button's
      aria-controls to point at. main-collection.liquid used to point at
      "filters", which was a class there and an id nowhere; it now
      carries CollectionFilters and the two are the same idea with two
      names, which is correct: two different pages, two different ids.
    - the price inputs carry onchange="this.form.submit()". Without it
      there is no way to apply a price at all; see the comment on those
      inputs. The shared rail has since adopted it.
    - the active chip. This file used to mark it with aria-pressed on the
      <label>, which the shared rail has now dropped for a plain .is-on
      class, and dropping it is right: aria-pressed only means anything
      on something with role="button", so on a label nothing read it and
      it was styling in an ARIA attribute's clothes. The checkbox inside
      already reports `checked`, which is the same fact announced
      properly. This file follows, so one rule set dresses both rails.
    - the chips draw their focus ring on :focus-within, in the
      stylesheet, because the thing that takes focus is the off-screen
      checkbox inside the label.
  One more is in the stylesheet: the phone drawer goes visibility:hidden
  when it is shut, so its checkboxes are not still in the tab order
  under the bottom of the screen.

  WHAT IS ABOVE THE GRID: the site's breadcrumb, then the collection
  name. Nothing else. No kicker, no description, no result count, no
  sort control. The previous Lada build was rejected for exactly this:
  too much writing, too many options. The reference the client gave,
  faizasaqlain.pk, puts one word above a wall of photographs. If a future
  pass wants to add a LINE here, that is the line to argue about first.

  The breadcrumb is not that line. It is the same <nav class="crumb">
  every other page on this store opens with, it is navigation rather
  than writing, and its absence was a large part of why Lada read as a
  different website instead of a room inside this one.

  THE RAIL IS DROPPED, NOT EMPTIED, WHEN THERE ARE NO FACETS.
  Search & Discovery populates collection.filters from the products in the
  collection, so a collection with nothing in it returns zero filter
  groups. The shared section still renders the <aside> in that state: a
  248px column holding a heading and no controls, standing next to an
  empty grid. Here the aside is not emitted at all and the grid takes the
  full measure, so the page has one empty thing on it instead of two.

  enabled_on TEMPLATES: COLLECTION. The section carries a preset, and a
  preset can be dropped on any JSON template from the theme editor. On a
  page or the home page `collection` is nil, and `paginate nil` and
  `nil > 0` both print a Liquid error where the grid should be. The
  restriction keeps it out of the picker everywhere it cannot work.

  EMPTY IS THE SHIPPING STATE. Both Lada collections have zero products
  today and the client loads them himself after handover. One line and a
  WhatsApp route, no plate, no heading of its own, nothing that has to be
  written again when the products land.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign heading = section.settings.heading | default: collection.title

  # Two different questions, and the page needs both.
  #
  #   has_filters  is there a rail to draw at all. False on an empty
  #                  collection, which is every Lada collection today.
  #   filters_on   has the customer actually narrowed something. Only
  #                  ever true when has_filters is true, and it is what
  #                  separates "nothing has been listed yet" from "your
  #                  filters matched nothing", which are the same empty
  #                  grid and completely different sentences.
  #
  # active_values covers list, boolean and swatch facets. A price range
  # has no active_values, it reports through min_value / max_value, so it
  # is asked separately or a customer who has set only a price ceiling
  # gets told the shop is empty.
  assign has_filters = false
  if collection.filters.size > 0
    assign has_filters = true
  endif

  assign filters_on = false
  for filter in collection.filters
    if filter.active_values.size > 0
      assign filters_on = true
    endif
    if filter.type == 'price_range'
      if filter.min_value.value != blank or filter.max_value.value != blank
        assign filters_on = true
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  assign wa      = section.settings.whatsapp | default: '923019090590'
  assign wa_text = section.settings.whatsapp_text | default: 'I would like to ask about'

  # The collection name is appended rather than typed into the message,
  # because one section file serves both Alamgeer and Ready to Wear and
  # the shopkeeper needs to know which one the message came from without
  # the client having to keep two copies of the same sentence in step.
  assign wa_msg = wa_text | append: ' ' | append: collection.title | append: '.'

  # Only the tail of the empty heading is a setting. The collection name
  # is prepended in the markup for the same reason it is appended to the
  # WhatsApp message above: one file serves both Alamgeer and Ready to
  # Wear, so a client who typed the whole sentence would have to keep two
  # copies of it in step. It is also how the white shop's own empty block
  # builds the line, and matching that block word for word is the point.
  assign empty_head = section.settings.empty_head | default: 'arriving shortly'

  # Card strings live on the section, not in the snippet, so the client
  # edits them once for the whole grid instead of once per product.
  assign quickview_label = section.settings.quickview_label | default: 'Quick view'
  assign add_label       = section.settings.add_label       | default: 'Add to bag'
  assign sold_label      = section.settings.sold_label      | default: 'Sold out'
-%}

<div class="wrap lcoll">

  {%- comment -%}
    Same element, same classes, same separator and same aria-label as
    sections/main-collection.liquid, so one rule set dresses both.
    .crumb--lada is the only addition: the shared rule paints
    --text-tertiary, a dark grey that disappears on a dark ground, so the
    modifier is the hook the Lada palette hangs off.

    The middle step is the Lada page itself, which is the collection's
    real parent and the thing the customer came through. It is composed
    from routes.root_url the way snippets/kj-nav.liquid and
    snippets/kj-footer.liquid already compose it, rather than looked up
    by handle: this trail is two links and must never be the reason a
    page half-renders.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <nav class="crumb crumb--lada lcoll__crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="{{ routes.root_url }}">Home</a><i>/</i>
    <a href="{{ routes.root_url }}pages/lada">Lada</a><i>/</i>
    <span aria-current="page">{{ collection.title }}</span>
  </nav>

  {%- comment -%}
    One h1 per page, and it is the collection name. Uppercase, if the
    client ever wants it, comes from text-transform in the stylesheet,
    never from capitals typed into the heading setting, because a
    setting value typed in capitals is stuck that way in search results,
    in the browser tab and in every screen reader that spells out
    all-caps words letter by letter.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="lcoll__head">
    <h1 class="lcoll__h1">{{ heading }}</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="listing lcoll__listing{% unless has_filters %} lcoll__listing--wide{% endunless %}">

    {%- if has_filters -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The id is new. main-collection.liquid points aria-controls at
        "filters" and nothing on that page carries that id, so the
        relationship it announces does not resolve. Fixed here rather
        than there because that file is not this task's to touch.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <aside class="filters" id="LadaFilters" data-filters aria-label="Filters">
        <div class="filters__bar">
          <h2 class="filters__h">{{ section.settings.filters_title | default: 'Filter' }}</h2>
          <button class="iconbtn filters__close" type="button" data-filter-close
                  aria-label="Close filters">&times;</button>
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          Identical to the shared rail. The checkbox is a real checkbox
          kept off-screen with .vh rather than opacity:0, so it stays in
          the tab order and still reports checked state; the label is the
          44px target around it. onchange submit is how the shared rail
          applies a facet without JS of its own, and Shopify's own
          filtering reads the resulting query string.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <form id="FacetFilterForm">
          {%- for filter in collection.filters -%}
            <div class="filters__grp">
              <h3>{{ filter.label }}</h3>
              <div class="filters__list" data-facet>
                {%- if filter.type == 'price_range' -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    onchange IS NOT DECORATION HERE, IT IS THE ONLY WAY TO
                    APPLY A PRICE.

                    This form has no submit button: the checkboxes post
                    themselves through onchange and the only button in the
                    rail, "View results", is type="button" and just shuts the
                    phone drawer. HTML's implicit submission then refuses to
                    fire, because a form with no submit button only submits on
                    Enter when it holds ONE field that blocks implicit
                    submission and a price range is two number inputs. So on
                    the shared rail a customer can type 2000 into Min, press
                    Enter, and nothing happens, on a phone and on a desktop
                    alike. The checkbox idiom one branch below is the fix, on
                    the same event: change fires when the value is committed,
                    which is Enter or leaving the field.

                    main-collection.liquid had the same defect and has
                    since taken the same fix, so the two rails now apply
                    a price the same way.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <div class="filters__price">
                    <input type="number" inputmode="numeric" name="{{ filter.min_value.param_name }}"
                           placeholder="Min" value="{{ filter.min_value.value }}" aria-label="Minimum price"
                           onchange="this.form.submit()">
                    <span aria-hidden="true">&ndash;</span>
                    <input type="number" inputmode="numeric" name="{{ filter.max_value.param_name }}"
                           placeholder="Max" value="{{ filter.max_value.value }}" aria-label="Maximum price"
                           onchange="this.form.submit()">
                  </div>
                {%- else -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    .is-on, not aria-pressed, and identical to the shared
                    rail's chip. See the note in this file's header.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- for value in filter.values -%}
                    <label class="chip{% if value.active %} is-on{% endif %}">
                      <input type="checkbox" class="vh" name="{{ value.param_name }}" value="{{ value.value }}"
                             {% if value.active %}checked{% endif %} onchange="this.form.submit()">
                      {{ value.label }} <span class="chip__n">{{ value.count }}</span>
                    </label>
                  {%- endfor -%}
                {%- endif -%}
              </div>
            </div>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </form>

        {%- comment -%}
          Phone only. site.css hides .filters__done from 1000px up, where
          the rail is a column on the page and there is nothing to close.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="filters__done">
          <button class="btn-lada" type="button" data-filter-close>{{ section.settings.filters_done | default: 'View results' }}</button>
        </div>
      </aside>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="lcoll__main">

      {%- comment -%}
        What is left of the toolbar: the button that opens the rail on a
        phone. The shared one also prints "N products", which is the
        result count the client struck out, and on a range this size a
        count only tells the customer how short it is. The row is hidden
        from 1000px up, so on a desktop nothing sits between the name and
        the photographs.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if has_filters -%}
        <div class="lcoll__bar">
          <button class="btn-lada btn-lada--ghost" type="button"
                  data-filter-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="LadaFilters">{{ section.settings.filters_button | default: 'Filter' }}</button>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- paginate collection.products by 24 -%}
        {%- if collection.products.size == 0 -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            Two empty states, because they are two different facts.

            THE FIRST ONE IS NOW THE WHITE SHOP'S, WORD FOR WORD, and
            that is the change. The two halves of this store used to
            describe the same situation in two unrelated voices: over
            there a collection was "arriving shortly" and being
            "photographed and listed now"; here it said "Nothing listed
            here yet", which is the same fact told as a shortage. A
            customer who walks from one listing to the other inside a
            minute reads two different shops. So this is the shared
            .empty block's heading and sentence, in the dark palette,
            and the heading comes back with it: the white shop repeats
            the collection name under its own h1 too, and matching is
            the whole point.

            ONE CLAUSE IS DROPPED AND ONLY ONE. The shared sentence ends
            "The filters are live, so as soon as pieces are added they
            will appear here." On this page there is no rail to point
            at: the aside is not rendered at all while a collection is
            empty, because Search & Discovery builds the facets out of
            the products in it. Promising a customer a filter he cannot
            see is worse than saying less.

            The WhatsApp button stays. It is the one way out of an empty
            room that this shop actually has, the client owns both the
            number and the wording in the editor, and deleting it would
            delete settings he has already saved.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="lcoll__empty">
            {%- if filters_on -%}
              <p class="lcoll__empty-p">{{ section.settings.no_match_text | default: 'Nothing here with those filters.' }}</p>
              <a class="lada__link" href="{{ collection.url }}">{{ section.settings.no_match_label | default: 'Show everything' }}</a>
            {%- else -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                h2, where the shared block uses h3. Over there an h3 sits
                under the rail's own "Filter" h2; here the rail is not
                rendered at all while the collection is empty, so an h3
                would jump straight from the page h1 to level three with
                nothing in between. The class is what the stylesheet
                hangs off, so the level can differ without the two
                blocks looking different.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <h2 class="lcoll__empty-h">{{ collection.title }} {{ empty_head }}</h2>
              <p class="lcoll__empty-p">{{ section.settings.empty_text | default: 'Stock is being photographed and listed now. As soon as pieces are added they will appear here.' }}</p>
              <a class="btn-lada" href="https://wa.me/{{ wa }}?text={{ wa_msg | url_encode }}"
                 target="_blank" rel="noopener">{{ section.settings.empty_cta | default: 'Ask on WhatsApp' }}</a>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>

        {%- else -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            .pgrid, the shared grid, on purpose: 2 columns, 3 from 760px,
            4 from 1100px, the same as every other listing on the store.
            The client asked for the listings to match and this is the
            single line that keeps them matching, so a Lada card lands on
            the same column width and the same 4:5 plate as a .pcard.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="pgrid lcoll__grid">
            {%- for product in collection.products -%}
              {% render 'lada-product-card', product: product, index: forloop.index0,
                 quickview_label: quickview_label, add_label: add_label, sold_label: sold_label %}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>

          {%- if paginate.pages > 1 -%}
            <nav class="pagination lcoll__pages" aria-label="Pagination">
              {{ paginate | default_pagination }}
            </nav>
          {%- endif -%}

        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endpaginate -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The scrim is only emitted alongside a rail. The shared section
      prints it unconditionally, which on a filterless collection leaves
      a third child in the .listing grid that nothing can ever open.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if has_filters -%}
      <div class="scrim" data-scrim></div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  THE QUICK VIEW PANEL, ONCE PER PAGE AND ONLY WHERE THERE IS SOMETHING
  TO OPEN IT.

  snippets/lada-product-card.liquid emits the trigger, this section owns
  the panel. That split is deliberate: the panel carries ids, so one per
  page, and the card is rendered once per product. Any section that
  renders the Lada card has to carry this line too.

  Gated on products_count rather than rendered unconditionally. On an
  empty collection, or on a filter that matched nothing, the grid emits
  no triggers at all, and a <dialog> with three ids in it that nothing
  on the page can ever open is markup for a state that does not exist.
  products_count respects the active filters, which is exactly the
  question being asked.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if collection.products_count > 0 -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    sold_label is passed the same string the cards were given. The panel
    writes it over its add button when a chosen colour or size is gone,
    and it defaults to "Sold out" on its own, so leaving it out did not
    break anything visibly. It did mean a client who renamed the label in
    the one place the theme editor offers it got the new word on every
    card in the grid and the old one inside the panel that opens off
    those cards.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'lada-quick-view',
     qv_section_id: section.settings.qv_section_id,
     close_label: section.settings.qv_close_label,
     loading_label: section.settings.qv_loading_label,
     error_text: section.settings.qv_error_text,
     full_label: section.settings.qv_full_label,
     sold_label: sold_label,
     fallback_title: section.settings.qv_title %}
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Lada collection",
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "Above the grid this page shows the collection name and nothing else. No description, no result count, no sort control. The photographs are the design." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "info": "Leave empty to use the collection name. One word where possible." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Product cards" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "These labels apply to every card in the grid. Type them in sentence case: the capitals on the page come from the stylesheet." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "quickview_label", "label": "Quick view button", "default": "Quick view" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "add_label", "label": "Add button", "default": "Add to bag" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "sold_label", "label": "Sold out label", "default": "Sold out" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Quick view panel" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The panel that opens from a card. It pulls the live product page in, so nothing in it is a second copy of anything." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "qv_title", "label": "Panel title before the product loads", "default": "Quick view" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "qv_close_label", "label": "Close button", "default": "Close" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "qv_loading_label", "label": "While it loads", "default": "Loading" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "qv_error_text", "label": "If it does not load", "default": "This did not load." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "qv_full_label", "label": "Link to the product page", "default": "See the full page" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "qv_section_id", "label": "Product section key", "default": "main", "info": "The name of the main section inside the product template. Leave this alone unless the product template names it something other than main." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Filter rail" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "The filters come from the Search & Discovery app and appear on their own once this collection has products in it. While it is empty there is no rail and the photographs take the full width." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "filters_title", "label": "Rail heading", "default": "Filter" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "filters_button", "label": "Phone open button", "default": "Filter" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "filters_done", "label": "Phone close button", "default": "View results" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "While the collection is empty" },
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "This is what the page shows until products are added, so it is the state it ships in. It says the same thing, in the same words, as every other collection on the store." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "empty_head", "label": "Heading", "default": "arriving shortly", "info": "The collection name is added in front of it, so this reads as Alamgeer arriving shortly." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "empty_text", "label": "Line", "default": "Stock is being photographed and listed now. As soon as pieces are added they will appear here." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "empty_cta", "label": "Button", "default": "Ask on WhatsApp" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "whatsapp", "label": "WhatsApp number", "default": "923019090590" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "whatsapp_text", "label": "Pre-filled message", "default": "I would like to ask about", "info": "The collection name is added to the end, so one message serves both Lada collections." },
    { "type": "header", "content": "When a filter matches nothing" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "no_match_text", "label": "Line", "default": "Nothing here with those filters." },
    { "type": "text", "id": "no_match_label", "label": "Link", "default": "Show everything" }
  ],
  "enabled_on": { "templates": ["collection"] },
  "presets": [
    { "name": "Lada collection" }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
